{% if _preset == 'Production' -%}
## Required: Codex second opinion (Production — always mandatory)

You **MUST** invoke Codex CLI as a second-LLM reviewer on **every** run —
on the Production preset this is mandatory whenever `codex_second_opinion.enabled`,
not opt-in. Run the invocation below, then reconcile **every** Codex finding.
{%- else -%}
## Required on high-diff: Codex second opinion (Side — mandatory iff high-blast-radius)

On the Side preset Codex is mandatory **only on high-diff changes** (PLAN-crossmodel-codex-gaps
ADR-002/003). First classify the diff — use `HEAD` (the diff under review may be staged)
and `--numstat` for the added-line count that drives the `boundary` signal:

```bash
files=$(git diff --name-only HEAD); added=$(git diff --numstat HEAD | awk '{s+=$1} END{print s+0}'); printf '%s\n' "$files" | python -m harness_maker.high_diff classify --added-lines "$added"
```

If `is_high` is true — or `boundary` is true and your judgment (reusing the review
When-to-Run criteria: >3 files, security/auth/perms, contract/architecture surface,
new public API) says high — you **MUST** invoke Codex below and reconcile **every**
finding. Otherwise the call is optional (opt-in per finding).
{%- endif %}
{%- else -%}
## Optional: Codex second opinion

You **MAY** invoke Codex CLI as a second-LLM reviewer when you reach a
finding that benefits from cross-model judgment (M-of-N consensus,
tie-break, architectural sanity check). This is opt-in per call — no
ambient Claude self-dispatch. (Heterogeneous-consensus mandatory wiring for
reviewers lands in PLAN-crossmodel-codex-gaps Phase 4b.)
{%- endif %}

**Invocation contract** (hermetic by default per ADR-006 — `--ignore-user-config`
and `--ignore-rules` strip developer-specific Codex AGENTS.md so the
second opinion is reproducible across machines):

```bash
tmp=$(mktemp)
# Build the prompt body in a tmpfile so adversarial diff content
# (e.g. a bare `PROMPT_END` line) CANNOT terminate a heredoc early
# (security-reviewer P1: heredoc terminator injection via reviewed diff).
prompt_tmp=$(mktemp)
# IMPORTANT: write the question / diff context to "$prompt_tmp" using
# `printf '%s' "$content" > "$prompt_tmp"` — never inline the content in
# this heredoc and never use eval/sh -c on it.
codex exec \
  --sandbox read-only \
  {%- if _csoo.get('hermetic', true) %}
  --ignore-user-config --ignore-rules \
  {%- endif %}
  --json \
  --output-schema "{{ _csoo.get('output_schema_path', '.claude/schemas/codex-finding.schema.json') }}" \
  --output-last-message "$tmp" \
  - < "$prompt_tmp"
status=$?
if [ "$status" -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "[codex second-opinion] skipped: exit=$status (proceeding Claude-only)" >&2
else
  cat "$tmp"  # JSON conforming to the configured output_schema_path
fi
rm -f "$tmp" "$prompt_tmp"
```

{% if name == 'plan-validator' -%}
**Output contract — this EXTENDS the `## Output JSON Schema` above.** Despite
its "Return ONLY this JSON" wording, when Codex is enabled your returned object
**MUST** carry two **additional top-level** keys alongside `overall_assessment`,
`critiques`, and `clean_categories` (NOT nested inside `critiques`):
- `codex_status`: `"invoked"` when the call succeeded, `"skipped"` when it failed.
- `codex_reconciliation`: an array, one entry per Codex finding —
  `{ "codex_finding_ref": "...", "disposition": "accepted" | "rejected" | "duplicate" | "unresolved", "reason": "..." }`.
  Empty array `[]` when `codex_status` is `"skipped"`.

**Anti-boilerplate floor** — every entry's `codex_finding_ref` MUST cite the
specific Codex finding it dispositions: its `file:line` (when present) or a
verbatim quote of the Codex finding `message`. A bare `"rejected: n/a"` with
no reference does NOT satisfy the contract — the mandatory call is a forced
*consideration*, not a decorative one. Your `overall_assessment` stays your
own Claude-derived verdict (Codex is input you cannot silently discard, not a
verdict source — preserves ADR-005 of PLAN-codex-second-llm-integration).

**PIDA debate flow (ADR-004, PLAN-crossmodel-codex-gaps)** — do NOT self-adjudicate
a Codex challenge silently. For each Codex finding:
1. Form a **rebuttal**: `KEEP` (the finding stands — disposition `accepted`) or
   `REFUTE` (with concrete evidence — disposition `rejected`/`duplicate`).
2. If a **test/oracle** can settle the disagreement, let it decide and record the
   oracle result in `reason`.
3. **No-oracle short-circuit:** plan-validator works on a *plan* — there is **no test oracle**
   here — so when KEEP/REFUTE cannot be settled by evidence, do NOT spend a
   wasted rebuttal pass pretending to arbitrate: set disposition `unresolved` and
   surface it. `[unresolved]` is visible-by-design (visibility > silent dismissal)
   and **never blocks** — `overall_assessment` is still your Claude verdict
   (warn-and-proceed). `/hm:plan` Step 4 shows `unresolved` entries to the user.

**Failure policy** (ADR-003 — warn-and-proceed, made loud): on any non-zero
exit (auth, rate-limit, network, schema rejection), do NOT block. Set
`codex_status: "skipped"`, `codex_skip_reason: "<one-line cause>"`, and
`codex_reconciliation: []`, then proceed with Claude-only analysis. `/hm:plan`
Step 4 surfaces the skip reason to the user.
{%- else -%}
**Output schema**: `{ findings: [{file, line, severity, message, evidence}],
summary, confidence }` — see `{{ _csoo.get('output_schema_path', '.claude/schemas/codex-finding.schema.json') }}`.

**Failure policy** (ADR-003 — warn-and-proceed): on any non-zero exit
(auth, rate-limit, network, schema rejection), log one line to stderr
and continue with Claude-only output. Do NOT block the stage.
{%- endif %}

**Skip-receipt (ADR-005)** — best-effort: whenever the Codex call is skipped
(non-zero exit) OR you disposition a Codex finding, append one ledger row so the
skip-rate / disposition history is auditable over time. Pass each value as a
**separate `--flag` argument** (never inline an untrusted cause/finding-ref into a
shell-quoted JSON blob — `codex_ledger` builds the JSON in Python, so a quote or
`;` in the value is data, not shell — REVIEW security P1):

```bash
reason="<one-line cause>"   # from the codex exit/stderr; quoting-safe as a shell var
python -m harness_maker.codex_ledger emit --slug "<task-slug>" --stage "<review|plan>" --finding-ref "<file:line or n/a>" --disposition "<accepted|rejected|duplicate|unresolved>" --codex-status "<invoked|skipped>" --skip-reason "$reason"
```
(Omit `--skip-reason` on the `invoked` path.) Best-effort by design — a
Bash-tool-gated agent may be unable to emit; the `/hm:health` Codex smoke check is
the positive backstop (a silently-disabled Codex is caught there even with no receipt).

**Prerequisite**: user must have run `codex login` on their machine.
First call surfaces an auth error if missing.
{%- endif -%}
